 "productionNotes": "No source consulted for this page gives a production total for the S130, worldwide or for the United States, and the figures that do circulate are sales counts rather than build counts. For the first model year the Z Car Club Association and conceptcarz both give 86,007 units sold in the US, described as the all-time sales record for the Z line; Hagerty rounds the same year to about 86,000 sold by US Datsun dealers, and Curbside Classic gives approximately 86K produced for 1979 and 55K for 1983, with no year below 53,000. For the whole run, two separate Hagerty articles put US sales at over 330,000 and at nearly 332,000 across five model years. These agree with one another, but they are not independent of each other and none of them names an underlying Nissan document, so productionTotal is left null rather than filled with a sales figure. No split by body (two-seat against 2+2), by roof, by transmission or by Turbo against naturally aspirated cars was found in any source, so the page states no Turbo production figure. The one variant count that four sources repeat is the 10th Anniversary edition of 1980: 3,000 cars, 2,500 in black and gold and 500 in red and black, per Nissan's own US newsroom, the Z Car Club Association, a Sport Z magazine article reprinted on zhome.com and Curbside Classic. On price, conceptcarz is the only source found that gives a first-year US list price, and Hagerty is the only source for an end-of-run range; a second Hagerty article prints first-year prices in a form that does not read as a list price and is not used. No US list price for the 10th Anniversary edition was documented beyond a dealer's recollection in a club magazine, so none is given. Period road tests were not retrieved directly; the Car and Driver verdict quoted here comes through Hagerty, and NHTSA and EPA records for the 1979-1983 cars were not retrieved.",
